{
	"nodes": [
		{
			"id": "header",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## Pharmacology\n\nDomain hub: drug exposure, target action, adaptation, safety principles, drug classes and how treatment evidence is measured.\n\n[[Pharmacology Map|Open the domain map]] - snapshot of 2026-09-25; the map's *In this folder* section is the live view.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 2100,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g0",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Exposure",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 710
		},
		{
			"id": "s0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacology - Exposure|Exposure index]]**",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Half-life and steady state]]**\n*process* - 5 sources\n\nHalf-life, accumulation and steady state as exposure models, with lithium as the worked example of why timing and clearance matter.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacokinetics and ADME]]**\n*process* - 6 sources\n\nAbsorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, each linked to the clinical consequences it explains.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 786,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g1",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Action",
			"x": 420,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 964
		},
		{
			"id": "s1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacology - Action|Action index]]**",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacodynamics and receptors]]**\n*mechanism* - 6 sources\n\nDrug targets, agonism and antagonism, the gap between occupancy and clinical effect, and receptor-level adaptation.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Receptor adaptation tolerance and dependence]]**\n*process* - 5 sources\n\nHow repeated drug action changes the system, and the difference between tolerance, dependence and withdrawal.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 762,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Target binding and dose-response]]**\n*mechanism* - 7 sources\n\nAffinity, efficacy and potency: what binding measurements establish and what they leave open.",
			"x": 450,
			"y": 1040,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g2",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Classes",
			"x": 840,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1266
		},
		{
			"id": "s2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacology - Classes|Classes index]]**",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 88,
			"color": "#B8A4FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Antidepressant mechanisms]]**\n*mechanism* - 6 sources\n\nWhat antidepressants do at the synapse, why clinical change is slower than the pharmacology, and what that implies.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 508,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Antipsychotic mechanisms]]**\n*mechanism* - 8 sources\n\nD2 blockade as the time-scoped shared mechanism of the dopamine-blocking class, the pathways behind response, movement and endocrine effects, and the 2024 muscarinic alternative.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 762,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 242,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Drug classes and mechanisms overview]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nHow psychotropic drug classes are organised by target and mechanism, and what the class labels do and do not tell you.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 1064,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 194,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Stimulant and non-stimulant mechanisms]]**\n*mechanism* - 6 sources\n\nHow ADHD medications increase catecholamine signalling by different routes, and how their evidence is measured.",
			"x": 870,
			"y": 1318,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 218,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g3",
